# Compiled code runs an arm's body with an err in a bound parameter when a
# sibling arm has `_` in that position. The interpreter does not.
#
# Run from this directory: kanso run main.kso --interp (right); kanso build
#  main.kso && ./main (wrong). `kanso play` on a one-file version shows it
#  too.
#
# `checked [] 5` answers an err. `pick 125 grown` should not run any body: the
# matching arm binds `xs`, and chapter 04 says a function handed an err as an
# argument never runs.
#
# Expected (and what the interpreter prints): an endpoint report for
# "refused 5".
# Actual, dev and release builds: `size 6`. The `125` arm ran, and
# `length` was handed the err and answered a number.
#
# Renaming the sibling's `_` to a bound, used name (`fn pick _ xs` with
# `"other {length xs}"`) makes the compiled code agree with the interpreter.
# This is the same rule as bugs/wildcard-swallows-err: a `_` parameter accepts
# an err. Here it leaks into the arm next to it, and only in compiled code.
#
# Where it bit: the TOML decoder's `inline_delim cs 125 p slots` arm built the
# inline table from a failed insertion on the release build, and reported
# "`.` reads a field of a record, not <value>" instead of "duplicate key `b`".
fn checked xs n
  return err "refused {n}" if n > 2
  push xs n

fn pick 125 xs
  "size {size_of xs}"

fn pick _ _
  "other"

fn size_of xs
  length xs

pub fn demo n
  grown = checked [] n
  pick 125 grown
